{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he stole, etc.; concerning that he did not, etc.</b> It is difficult: how can it be justified to say of him that he was fleeing, given that it says, ״עַל בְּלִי הִגִּיד לוֹ כִּי בֹרֵחַ״? Perhaps the intention is that Jacob acted wisely when he recounted to Laban all his deeds and conduct: he concealed from him the stratagem of flight, and went further to remove it from Laban's mind by asking him to send him away. By this he justified Laban's thinking that he would not flee at all — for otherwise, why would he ask him to send him away? And in this he stole his heart. This is what the verse says: <b>and he stole</b>, etc. — and by what did he steal? On account of the fact that in the telling of his deeds he did not inform him that he planned to flee when necessity would require him to flee. And in this we find the essential reason for this verse's being stated, which comes to tell the reason by which the flight was accomplished — for without this, Laban would have set his mind to guard him from fleeing by any means whatsoever; but he stole his heart, and in this found the opportunity to gather all that he had and flee without anyone knowing. And this is what it says immediately afterward: ״וַיִּבְרַח הוּא וְכָל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ״, etc. Understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:17.863030155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}